#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9b29c-326d-576c-b998-ab975492ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form"}] :question/id #uuid "c9e4edff-b04f-5f6c-8066-43cae6a653e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1466" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-3(x+5)^2-4$?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n$$\ny=-3(x+5)^2-4\n$$\n\nis written in vertex form with $h=-5$ and $k=-4$. To see this clearly, note that we can write the parabola as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-3(x-(-5))^2-4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(-5,-4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087445 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4018be9-18ab-5854-87d6-149238ab6f53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form"}] :question/id #uuid "8b64498e-61fb-5eab-a97a-b321be8360ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-1795" :question/problem "What is the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=4(x-3)^2+2$?" :question/worked-solution "The axis of symmetry of the parabola is the vertical line that passes through the vertex of the parabola. To find the axis of symmetry, we need to compute the $x$-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nRemember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n$$\ny=4(x-3)^2+2\n$$\n\nis written in vertex form with $h=3$ and $k=2$. Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(3,2)$, and the axis of symmetry is $x=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97f63d2a-8297-5034-96e0-ecae4f62c5a8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum or Maximum Value of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form"}] :question/id #uuid "d4c1e4ea-da29-5027-bfc3-f909f9181cd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7479" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of the parabola $y=(x-5)^2 -4$, sketched below.\n\n![](images/e3/e360d812160d36bbc528ce65f0c8b19941e75ce8703204205a94967ecccdc892.png)" :question/worked-solution "The minimum value of the parabola is the $y$-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nRemember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n$$\ny = (x-5)^2 -4\n$$\n\nis written in vertex form with $h=5$ and $k=-4$. Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(5,-4)$, and its minimum value is $-4$."}]}
